BITNOB_FEE_TREASURY_BASE=""
BITNOB_FEE_TREASURY_POLYGON=""
BITNOB_FEE_TREASURY_ETHEREUM=""
BITNOB_FEE_TREASURY_ARBITRUM=""
BITNOB_FEE_TREASURY_OPTIMISM=""
BITNOB_FEE_TREASURY_AVALANCHE=""
BITNOB_FEE_TREASURY_SOLANA=""
BITNOB_FEE_TREASURY_STELLAR=""
BITNOB_FEE_TREASURY_ARC=""

# ===========================================
# ADMIN & OPERATIONS
# ===========================================
# Comma-separated. Grants access to /admin AND receives operational alerts —
# notably "refund owed", sent when a withdrawal fails after the user's deposit
# landed and their USDC has to be returned by hand.
# If this is empty nobody is alerted, and the failure is only visible in the logs.
ADMIN_EMAILS=""

# Bearer token the reconcile cron must present. Set it here AND in the scheduled
# job, or the endpoint is reachable by anyone.
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET" https://your-domain/api/cron/reconcile-transactions
# Run it every 2 minutes: it recovers withdrawals whose payout was never completed,
# and a payout quote only lives ~16 minutes.
CRON_SECRET=""

# Dune API key, Read/Write scope, created at https://dune.com/apis?tab=keys
# Create it in the same account context (personal vs team) that owns the dataset.
# Only used by /api/cron/sync-dune-roster, which publishes our wallet addresses so the public
# dashboard can scope on-chain queries to Sendzz users. Optional: leave it empty and the route
# returns 503 without doing anything. The same roster can be uploaded by hand from Dune's
# Create -> Upload data screen, so the dashboard does not depend on this being set.
# Daily is plenty — it only needs to catch new signups.
DUNE_API_KEY=""

# ===========================================
# SUPPORT
# ===========================================
# Telegram group or account the support widget hands users off to.
# Shown on the dashboard and the home page. Without it the button points at a
# placeholder that goes nowhere.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_TELEGRAM_URL=""

# ===========================================
# ENVIRONMENT
# ===========================================
# "mainnet" or "testnet" — which Supabase project and chain set the app runs against.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_NETWORK=""

# ===========================================
# TRANSACTION PIN
# ===========================================
# Server-side secret mixed into every PIN before it is hashed. A 4-digit PIN is only 10,000
# possibilities, so a stolen database alone would be enough to test every one of them — this
# is the piece that is NOT in the database, so a dump cannot even construct a guess.
#
# Generate once:   openssl rand -base64 32
# Minimum 16 characters. Below that the app refuses to hash a PIN at all rather than
# storing a weaker one.
#
# NEVER change it once users have PINs: every stored PIN is derived from it, so changing it
# locks everyone out at once with no way to recover. Treat it like the service role key.
#
# REQUIRED IN EVERY ENVIRONMENT. The PIN now gates every outgoing transaction, so a deployment
# without this cannot hash or verify one — which means nobody can send, withdraw or bridge.
# It is not an optional hardening step any more; the app is non-functional without it.
PIN_PEPPER=""

# The PIN reset path emails a code, and that code is encrypted at rest with TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY
# (set under the 2FA section above). Without it, a user who forgets their PIN has no way back
# in — so that variable is now load-bearing for recovery, not just for authenticator secrets.


# ===========================================
# REFERRALS
# ===========================================
# What a referrer earns, as a percentage of OUR REVENUE on the deposits made by people they
# referred — not a percentage of the deposits themselves.
#
# That distinction is the whole design. Paying a share of deposit principal would mean paying
# real money, out of pocket, to anyone willing to move the same balance in and out in a loop:
# an on-chain deposit costs the sender nothing but gas and earns us nothing. Sharing revenue
# cannot be farmed at a loss, because the payout is by construction a fraction of something we
# were paid first.
#
# Concretely: a referee deposits 1,000 USDC by bank transfer, PAYCREST_FEE_PERCENT is 1, so we
# earn 10 USDC. At REFERRAL_SHARE_PERCENT=20 the referrer gets 2 USDC.
#
# Unset or 0 disables accrual entirely — earnings simply stop being recorded. It fails quiet
# rather than loud on purpose: this is read inside the provider webhook that confirms a user's
# deposit, and that must never fail over a referral misconfiguration.
REFERRAL_SHARE_PERCENT="20"

# The balance a referrer has to reach before a payout is sent. Below this, earnings wait.
#
# Commissions are usually cents, and sending each one as its own USDC transfer would cost more
# in gas than the payment is worth. Defaults to 5 if unset.
REFERRAL_MIN_PAYOUT_USDC="5"

# ── Payout treasury (Circle developer-controlled wallet, on Base) ────────────
# The wallet referral payouts are sent FROM. This is a different thing from the fee treasury
# addresses above: those only RECEIVE, so an address is enough. Paying out means signing, which
# needs a wallet Circle holds the keys for.
#
# Until all four of these are set, the payout cron refuses to run and says so. Nothing is lost
# while it is unset — earnings keep accruing to the ledger, and the first configured run pays
# them. See the setup steps in the commit message for 053_referrals.sql.
#
#   1. Circle console → Wallets → create a wallet set (or reuse CIRCLE_WALLET_SET_ID)
#   2. Create a wallet on BASE in that set — note its id and address
#   3. Fund the address with USDC (and a little ETH for gas if not using a gas policy)
#   4. Look up the USDC token id for Base via Circle's /v1/w3s/tokens endpoint
CIRCLE_PAYOUT_WALLET_ID=""
# Circle's internal token id for USDC on Base — NOT the USDC contract address.
CIRCLE_PAYOUT_USDC_TOKEN_ID=""
